{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "wait-completion-order",
      "text": "Start with a zero-value WaitGroup and call Add(1) before launching the worker. If Wait registers before the worker calls Done, Done takes the counter to zero, clears the recorded waiter count, and releases each registered waiter. If Done finishes first, a later Wait sees zero and returns without acquiring the semaphore. A Wait that returns before the first Add does not cover the work added afterward.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 142,
        "fragment": "runtime_Semrelease(&wg.sema, false, 0)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "negative-counter",
      "text": "Start outside a synctest bubble with a zero-value WaitGroup and no pending tasks. Call Done once. Add(-1) stores a counter of -1 and then panics. If the caller recovers, the same WaitGroup still has a negative counter. A subsequent Wait registers as a waiter and blocks if there are no further operations.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 104,
        "fragment": "state := wg.state.Add(uint64(delta) << 32)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "reuse-before-wait-returns",
      "text": "Start outside synctest with one task and a registered waiter. If the task calls Done and the waiter returns before the next Add(1), the same WaitGroup can track a new batch. If Add(1) takes the counter from zero to one before the old waiter count is cleared, Add panics. If Add(1) runs after the state is cleared but before the old waiter checks it, the waiter panics if it sees nonzero state. If the entire second batch starts and finishes in that latter window, the old waiter can see zero and return even though the reuse violated the documented ordering.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 207,
        "fragment": "isReset := wg.state.Load() != 0"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "go-completion-and-goexit",
      "text": "Start with an empty WaitGroup and call Go(f) before Wait. Go adds the task before launching f, so f can register another task on the same WaitGroup while its own count is outstanding. When f returns normally or calls runtime.Goexit, f's deferred calls run before Go's deferred wrapper calls Done. Wait can then return once all registered tasks have decremented the counter. Wait itself has no cancellation operation.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 254,
        "fragment": "f completed normally, or abruptly using goexit."
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the Go contract explicitly say that runtime.Goexit also removes the task and clarify how the synchronization guarantee applies to that path?"
    },
    {
      "id": "go-escaping-panic",
      "text": "Start with a WaitGroup whose only outstanding task was started by Go, with a waiter already registered. If a panic escapes f and the wrapper recovers a non-nil value, the wrapper re-panics before reaching Done. The task count stays at one and the waiter receives no semaphore release from this task before the uncaught panic terminates the process. The comment's scenario in which calling Done would let the main goroutine exit first requires the decrement to complete the group and the main goroutine to be waiting on it.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 251,
        "fragment": "panic(x)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "synctest-association-after-completion",
      "text": "Start with an unassociated WaitGroup. In one synctest bubble, call Add(1) and then Done with no registered waiter. The task counter reaches zero, but the same WaitGroup remains associated with that bubble. If a Wait in the owning bubble clears the association first, a subsequent outside Add(1) can succeed. If the outside Add(1) comes first, it hits the mixed inside/outside fatal check. An outside Wait can return at zero without clearing the association. With a registered waiter at the final Done, Done clears the association before releasing the waiter, and ordinary reuse still requires the previous Wait to return.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 174,
        "fragment": "if w == 0 && state&waitGroupBubbleFlag != 0 && synctest.IsAssociated(wg) {"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Is the bubble association meant to persist after completion with no waiters until the owning bubble calls Wait?"
    }
  ]
}
